// R5: Viva Notes (struct vs class, protocol vs inheritance, map/filter/reduce)
// ============================================================================
/*
 1. struct vs. class:
    - Value vs. Reference: Structs are value types (copied on assignment/passing);
      classes are reference types (shared pointer to the same memory instance).
    - Memory & ARC: Structs live on the stack without ARC overhead; classes live on
      the heap and use ARC (susceptible to retain cycles).
    - Inheritance & Mutability: Classes support single inheritance; structs do not
      (they use protocols) and need the 'mutating' keyword to modify properties.

 2. protocol vs. Inheritance:
    - Composition vs. Hierarchy: Inheritance is a rigid single-parent "is-a" hierarchy
      for classes only. Protocols define a "can-do" blueprint usable by classes, structs,
      and enums.
    - Multiple Conformance: A class inherits from at most one superclass, whereas any
      type can conform to multiple protocols simultaneously.
    - Default Implementations: Protocol extensions provide shared behavior without tight
      parent-child coupling.

 3. map, filter, and reduce:
    - map: Transforms each element in a collection and returns a new array
      (e.g., [1, 2, 3].map { $0 * 2 } -> [2, 4, 6]).
    - filter: Returns a new array containing only elements matching a boolean predicate
      (e.g., [1, 2, 3, 4].filter { $0 % 2 == 0 } -> [2, 4]).
    - reduce: Combines all elements into a single accumulated value from an initial seed
      (e.g., [1, 2, 3, 4].reduce(0, +) -> 10).
 */

